Ladybird lays out dependent table-cell contents again after row sizes
are assigned, using the stretched content size as a definite percentage
basis. It retains the automatic content extent for alignment, and keeps
the assigned row sizes when percentage-sized contents overflow them.
Descendant dependency information reaches the table through each cell
layout result, including anonymous and table wrappers. It also covers
orthogonal contents whose available inline size depends on row height.

Blink separates the final cell size from its initial definiteness in
ComputeCellBlockSize. A fixed-height cell is definite, as is a cell that
grew when its table has a specified block size. The row algorithm uses
SetupTableCellConstraintSpaceBuilder to pass this state to the child
layout, which consumes it in ComputeInitialBlockSizeForFragment.
TableRowLayoutAlgorithm::Layout repeats percentage-dependent layouts
before determining the row baseline with RowBaselineTabulator.

WebKit repeats qualifying percentage-dependent layouts through its
RenderTableSection::relayoutCellIfFlexed method. The override comes from
RenderTableCell::setOverridingLogicalHeightFromRowHeight. Its baseline
update only raises the saved row baseline. Ladybird rebuilds affected
row baselines after every dependent cell has been laid out, so baselines
can shrink and alignment is independent of cell order, matching Chrome.

Gecko requests a special block-size reflow through its table and cell
frames. nsTableCellFrame::NotifyPercentBSize registers percentage-sized
contents, and nsTableFrame::RequestSpecialBSizeReflow marks ancestors.
nsTableCellFrame::Reflow obtains the spanned row size without borders
and padding through CalcUnpaginatedBSize. In standards mode, its method
NeedsToObserve restricts the descendant observer to table frames.

Runtime comparisons used Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit
26.5. Ladybird follows Chrome where they differ: Non-table children grow
in specified-height tables, even without a percentage height on the
cell. Intrinsic table heights also qualify. WebKit agrees on most such
cases, but differs for fixed-height cells beside taller siblings, some
anonymous-block cases, shrinking baselines, and repeated height changes.
Firefox restricts many standards-mode cases to table children. Both
vertical writing modes use the horizontal row extent for percentages.
Auto-sized orthogonal contents receive the final available row size,
but keep the viewport cap in orthogonal_flow_inline_size. Their size
agrees with Firefox and WebKit, and remains different from Chrome.
